  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $cos\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}, $\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi , & \\, & \\, & x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+n\\cdot 2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$",
